ituations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures.  Identifies general aspects related to Software Programming in short written or spoken conversations about past experiences.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob10re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Investigate about more characteristics and functions of the Kernel language ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write down your findings about extra information about Kernel language ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share your ideas with your teacher and classmates.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I investigate about Kernel language ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write information about Kernel language ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I talk about Kernel language with my teacher and classmates", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "core language ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "principal or main language", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(compound noun) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: but now I know that the Kernel language translates all language constructs from a core language to ease the understanding of the program functionality.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob10aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "efficiency ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the good use of time ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The first one defines a simple language, which is very easy to implement and also improves efficiency. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob10aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "translation scheme ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "an organized structure for translating ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The second one defines a translation scheme from all the programming languages to the kernel language.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob10aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "logical", "type": "name"}, {"content": "in acordance with the reasoning", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The logical semantics approach which defines a statement as a model of a logical theory.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob10aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "program", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a series of instructions for the computer to make it perform an operation", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: but now I know that the Kernel language translates all language constructs from a core language to ease the understanding of the program functionality.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob10aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "abstraction", "type": "name"}, {"content": "in linguistics, a high level of analysis ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: They are the namely linguistic abstraction and the syntactic sugar.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob10aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Simple past Tense in Affirmative, and Negative, Regular vs Irregular Verbs", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use <strong>the simple past tense</strong> to express actions that started and finished in the past.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You use the <strong>Simple Past</strong> tense to talk about a completed event in the past. You can form the simple past tense by adding <strong>ed</strong>, <strong>-ied</strong> to the regular verbs. For irregular verbs, you have to learn them by heart, as they change in the past. </br></br>  Example: </br></br>  <span class=\"italic\"> We <strong>designed</strong> a new program.</span>(regular verb) </br>        <span class=\"italic\"> He <strong>found</strong> new software.</span> (irregular verb) </br></br>           You need the auxiliary verb <strong>did</strong> for the negative and interrogative form; use <i>did</i> with all the subject pronouns. </br></br>  Example: </br></br> <span class=\"italic\"> We <strong>didn't</strong> design a new program.</span> (regular verb) </br>                <span class=\"italic\"><strong>Did</strong> they study here? </span></br> </br>  Notice that when you use the auxiliary <i>did</i>, the main verb takes the infinitive form. </br></br>  Look at the chart of the simple past.  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"\\t\\t Simple Past\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\"\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":4,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Affirmative \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Negative  \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Questions\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Regular verbs I/you/we/they/He/she/it\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I watched a new program. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"We <strong>didn't </strong>send new products.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Did</strong> they get products?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Irregular verbs I/you/we/they/He/she/it\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He sold a software.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"She<strong> didn't </strong>buy software. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Did</strong> she buy a computer?\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\"\"}", "type": "table"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Infinitives After Adjectives", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use an <strong>infinitive</strong> after <strong>adjectives</strong> to describe the action that causes that feeling in you; this is, to describe how you feel about a particular action.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Remember that <strong>adjectives</strong> are words that you use to describe the nouns. </br></br> Example: </br></br> <span class=\"italic\">This exercise is very <strong>easy</strong>.</span> </br> <span class=\"italic\">I feel <strong>happy</strong> to learn <strong>new</strong> things. </span> </br></br>  Infinitives almost always begin with the preposition <strong>to</strong> and after them you write the simple form of the verb, which is the verb with no conjugation. It means that for the infinitive verbs you don't have to add s, or es, ed or ing at the end. </br></br>  With the adjectives the structure is very simple.</br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> Subject+ verb to be+ <strong>adjective</strong>+ <strong>infinitive</strong></div> </br> Example: </br></br> <span class=\"italic\">That is <strong>easy to explain</strong>.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I am very glad to see you again.  ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I'm lucky to have you here to help me with this. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Those are simple translations to identify and they can have two kinds, namely linguistic abstraction and syntactic sugar.", "type": "example"}]}]}